-[^deep]: For example, one way to make values deep is to make them not linearly removable and entangled with capability. That means we learn them to be high curvature with respect to the general capabilities loss, so removing them also damages the model. This seems a straightforward modification to existing character and preference training. See [Springer et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15799) (alignment sits in a small, brittle subspace), [Merullo et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24256) (low-curvature directions can be edited out cheaply), and [Qi et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05946) (alignment is often only a few tokens deep). The same depth may make values harder for us to correct later, so this may trade robustness against correctability.
+[^deep]: For example, one possibility is to make values not linearly removable and entangled with capability. We could try learning them in directions of high curvature with respect to the general capabilities loss, hoping that removing them also damages the model. This is untested, and high capability curvature may also increase damage during training. Reversing a graft restores the original model, so this would need capabilities that depend on the learned values. See [Springer et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15799) (studies alignment-loss curvature and fine-tuning drift), [Merullo et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24256) (some low-curvature weight components can be edited out, with costs to facts and arithmetic), and [Qi et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05946) (alignment is often only a few tokens deep). The same depth may make values harder for us to correct later, so this may trade robustness against correctability. <!-- PI/gpt-6.1-sol: curvature claim qualified after primary-source review; unpublished edit. -->
 
